83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 70 0 R
/T (Abstract )
/E (Abstract )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Low-code application development has moved from a niche productivity aid to a foundational pillar of enterprise software delivery. This survey report examines the current state and emerging trajectories of low-code and no-code \(LCNC\) platforms, drawing on market research, adoption statistics, and platform capabilities observed through 2026. The report covers the historical evolution of low-code tooling, the technological forces reshaping it \(particularly generative and agentic artificial intelligence\), governance and security considerations for enterprise-scale deployment, comparative positioning of leading vendors, and the socio-economic implications of the citizen-developer movement. The objective is to provide a structured, evidence-based overview useful to students, practitioners, and decision-makers evaluating low-code adoption )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
85 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 70 0 R
/T (1. Introduction )
/E (1. Introduction )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
86 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (Application development has traditionally required specialized programming skills, lengthy development cycles, and significant engineering resources. As digital transformation initiatives accelerate across industries, organizations face mounting pressure to deliver software faster while managing a persistent shortage of skilled developers. Low-code application development platforms \(LCAPs\) address this gap by offering visual, model-driven environments that allow both professional developers and non-technical "citizen developers" to build functional applications with minimal hand-written code. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
87 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 70 0 R
/E (The term "low-code" refers to development environments that use visual interfaces, drag-and-drop components, and pre-built templates to abstract away much of the underlying code, while "no-code" platforms extend this further by removing the need to write any code at all. Together, these approaches are often grouped under the umbrella term LCNC \(low-code/no-code\). This report surveys the trends currently shaping this space and evaluates their significance for the future of software engineering. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
