100 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (This report is based on a review of industry analyst forecasts \(including Gartner and Forrester commentary reported in secondary sources\), vendor documentation, and published market research from 2025-2026. Statistics cited are drawn from multiple independent research and analytics publications to cross-validate trend directions rather than rely on a single source. The survey focuses on enterprise and mid-market low-code adoption, with particular attention to the integration of artificial intelligence into the development lifecycle. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
101 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Multiple industry analysts converge on the conclusion that low-code has become a core layer of enterprise technology rather than a peripheral experiment. The following figures, compiled from several independent 2026 industry reports, illustrate the scale of this shift. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
102 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (The conceptual roots of low-code trace back to fourth-generation programming languages \(4GLs\) and rapid application development \(RAD\) tools of the 1990s, which sought to reduce the manual coding burden through visual modeling. Over the following two decades, the approach matured through business process management \(BPM\) suites, and later through dedicated cloud-native platforms such as OutSystems, Mendix, and Microsoft Power Apps. The modern wave of low-code, however, is distinguished by three factors: cloud-native scalability, deep integration with enterprise data and APIs, and, most recently, the infusion of artificial intelligence throughout the build process. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
103 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Where earlier platforms were largely confined to internal tools and departmental workflows, contemporary )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (low-code platforms are now used to build customer-facing applications, complex workflow automations, and even components of mission-critical enterprise systems. This shift reflects growing confidence in the maturity, security, and scalability of low-code tooling among enterprise IT leadership. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
