/P 76 0 R
/E (Beyond aggregate statistics, it is useful to consider the categories of applications most commonly built using low-code platforms in practice. Internal operational dashboards that aggregate data from multiple enterprise systems into a single view for managers are among the most frequently cited use cases, since they require rapid iteration and close alignment with evolving business questions rather than long-term architectural stability. Approval and workflow automation applications, such as expense approvals, leave requests, and vendor onboarding processes, represent another common category, as these tend to follow well-defined logical steps that map naturally onto the visual, flow-based paradigms that low-code platforms are built around. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
185 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (Customer-facing applications represent a more advanced and rapidly growing use case category, particularly in financial services and retail, where organizations use low-code platforms to launch minimum viable digital products quickly and then iterate based on user feedback. Data collection and field-service applications, often deployed on mobile devices, are also widely built using low-code tooling, taking advantage of built-in offline synchronization and device-integration features that would otherwise require substantial custom engineering effort to replicate. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
