197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (As low-code platforms take on a larger share of enterprise application delivery, the security posture of these platforms has come under closer scrutiny from both vendors and enterprise buyers. A growing area of investment is the ability to apply consistent security policies, such as data-access controls, authentication standards, and encryption requirements, uniformly across applications regardless of whether they were built by a professional engineering team or a citizen developer in a business unit. Centralized administration consoles now commonly provide visibility into every application built on the platform, along with the ability to flag or restrict applications that access sensitive data categories without proper approval. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (A recurring question for technology leaders is not whether to use low-code at all, but where it fits alongside traditional, hand-coded software engineering. The two approaches are increasingly viewed as complementary rather than competing. Traditional development remains the preferred approach for highly complex, performance-critical, or deeply customized systems where fine-grained control over architecture and behavior is essential, such as core transaction-processing engines, real-time trading systems, or specialized scientific computing applications. Low-code, in contrast, excels where speed, accessibility, and iterative business alignment matter more than granular control, such as internal workflow tools, customer onboarding portals, and departmental data applications. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 78 0 R
/E (The distinction is increasingly one of degree rather than kind. Many organizations now adopt a hybrid strategy in which low-code platforms handle the majority of routine application development, while professional engineering teams focus on the underlying platforms, APIs, and integration layers that low-code applications consume. This division of labor allows scarce engineering talent to be directed toward the highest-value technical work, while business teams retain the ability to build and adapt their own tools without waiting in an IT backlog queue. )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
